---
// What the website and the app collect and connect to. Keep in step with the code (updates.rs, icon.rs,
// tauri.conf.json) and the site (analytics in Site.astro and astro.config.mjs).
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import FinePrint from '../components/FinePrint.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---
<Site page="privacy">
  <FinePrint title="Privacy" updated="2026-11-15">
    <p>Short version: Penates has no account and no telemetry. It never sends anything about you or your apps to us. This website counts page views without cookies.</p>

    <h2>The App</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>No account, no telemetry.</b> Penates does not report what you do, which apps you have, or anything else.</li>
      <li><b>Your apps stay on your computer.</b> Each app's settings, icon, cookies and logins live in its own folder on your computer. Exports (<code>.penates</code> files) contain settings and icons, never cookies or logins.</li>
      <li><b>What it connects to:</b>
        <ul>
          <li>The sites you turn into apps, when you use them, and when you create or update an app (to read its name and icon).</li>
          <li>GitHub, to check for and download Penates updates. The manager checks when it starts (you can turn that off in Settings) and when you press Check. App windows never check.</li>
          <li>GitHub, for the app catalog: when you open the Catalog tab, create an app, or check your apps for updates. In Settings you can turn it off and use catalogs of your own, which are read from the addresses you give.</li>
          <li>The sites in the Catalog tab, to show their icons (kept for 30 days, then fetched again).</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><b>Sharing with the catalog</b> opens GitHub in your browser with the entry filled in; Penates itself sends nothing.</li>
      <li><b>A log on your computer.</b> Penates writes its own warnings, errors and crashes to a small log file in its data folder (at most about half a megabyte). It is never sent anywhere; you can delete it from Settings.</li>
      <li><b>Report a problem</b> shows the Penates version, your system and the recent log lines, with folder paths, your user name, email and IP addresses and everything after the site in web addresses removed, then opens GitHub in your browser with them filled in. Nothing is sent until you submit the issue there, and issues on GitHub are public.</li>
      <li>The sites you use through Penates have their own privacy policies; Penates does not change what they collect.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>This Website</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Page counts without cookies:</b> Cloudflare Web Analytics records page views, the page you came from, your country and your kind of device, without cookies and without following you across sites.</li>
      <li><b>No cookies.</b> If you pick light or dark in the docs, your browser remembers it locally; it is never sent to us.</li>
      <li><b>Hosting:</b> the site is served by Cloudflare, which handles requests like any web host (including your IP address).</li>
      <li><b>Download buttons</b> ask GitHub which files the latest release has, so your browser contacts GitHub, where the downloads come from anyway.</li>
      <li>The <a href={base + 'demo/'}>demo</a> runs entirely in your browser; nothing you do there is saved or sent.</li>
      <li>The fonts are served from this site, not from Google.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Email</h2>
    <p>Mail to <a href="mailto:hello@getpenates.com">hello@getpenates.com</a> is forwarded to the maintainer's inbox and used only to answer you.</p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>Write to <a href="mailto:hello@getpenates.com">hello@getpenates.com</a> or open an issue on <a href="https://github.com/Natetronn/penates/issues">GitHub</a>.</p>
  </FinePrint>
</Site>
